drm_sysctl: detect PCI by ancestry, not dev_is_pci()

The guard added to drm_add_busid_modesetting() fixed a real panic -- to_pci_dev()
is a container_of and vc4_firmware_kms was dereferencing it on a device-tree
device -- but it guards on a predicate that is never true, so it took every PCI
GPU down the platform path with it.

dev_is_pci() tests the devclass of the device ITSELF:

	device_get_devclass(dev->bsddev) == devclass_find("pci")

and a LinuxKPI DRM device is never in the "pci" devclass. i915, amdgpu, radeon,
bochs and vboxvideo all set their pci_driver name to "drmn" so LinuxKPI routes
them to the vgapci devclass, and lkpifill_pci_dev() points bsddev at that drmn
child. The test is a constant false for every DRM device on FreeBSD.

So hw.dri.0.busid shipped as "platform:drmn0" for an Intel GPU at 0000:00:02.0.

That sysctl is the only channel by which X learns a DRM device's PCI address on
FreeBSD. libudev-devd reads hw.dri.<N>.busid, rewrites a "pci:" prefix to
"pci-" and publishes it as ID_PATH; xorg's config/udev.c looks for "pci-",
finds none, and leaves attribs->busid NULL -- and its !value fallback does not
fire either, because the value is non-NULL. pd->pdev stays NULL,
xf86_check_platform_slot() cannot match the already-claimed PCI entity, the card
is claimed a second time as a GPU screen, and drmSetMaster returns EBUSY on a
device the primary screen already holds master on:

	(EE) modeset(G0): drmSetMaster failed: Device busy
	Fatal server error:
	AddScreen/ScreenInit failed for gpu driver 0 -1

LoginWindow's KeepAlive then restarts X forever.

Measured on one ThinkPad T460s, same GPU, same xlibre-server-25.1.9:

	stock FreeBSD 15.1:  hw.dri.0.busid = pci:0000:00:02.0
	                     Platform device "/dev/dri/card0" at 0@0:2:0 can be claimed.
	                     ... skipped because ... already been claimed   -> X starts
	NextBSD:             hw.dri.0.busid = platform:drmn0
	                     Platform device "/dev/dri/card0" can be claimed.
	                     -> modeset(G0) -> drmSetMaster EBUSY -> fatal

Everything else was held constant and cleared by experiment on that one machine:
XLibre version, scfb and vesa being installed, AutoAddGPU, the GPU itself, and
drm-kmod 6.12 (built from ports against the running kernel -- stock with 6.12
starts X fine).

What separates the two cases is ANCESTRY, not the device's own class:

	amd64, i915:   pci0 -> vgapci0 -> drmn0
	arm64, vc4:    ofwbus0 -> simplebus1 -> vc40

both read off live machines. So walk up until a PCI bus turns up. A device-tree
device never reaches one, which keeps the container_of out of reach for it and
preserves the panic fix -- VideoCore6KMS and V3D are still non-PCI, so simply
assuming PCI the way upstream drm-kmod does ("BSDFIXME: Assume it's PCI for
now", drm_ioctl.c) would reintroduce the panic on the Pi.

Blast radius of the bug being fixed: the patch applies to drm.ko itself on both
arches, so the busid was wrong for i915, amdgpu, radeon, bochs and vboxvideo --
and for anything else reading that sysctl, including libdrm/Mesa device
enumeration and xf86-video-* BusID matching.

--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_sysctl_freebsd.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/drm_sysctl_freebsd.c
@@ -165,21 +165,80 @@
 	return (-error);
 }
 
+/*
+ * Does this DRM device hang off a PCI bus?
+ *
+ * dev_is_pci() cannot answer that. It tests the devclass of the device ITSELF,
+ *
+ *	device_get_devclass(dev->bsddev) == devclass_find("pci")
+ *
+ * and a LinuxKPI DRM device is never in the "pci" devclass. i915, amdgpu,
+ * radeon, bochs and vboxvideo all set their pci_driver name to "drmn" so that
+ * LinuxKPI routes them to the vgapci devclass (linux_pci.c: isdrm =
+ * strcmp(pdrv->name, "drmn") == 0), and lkpifill_pci_dev() then points bsddev
+ * at that drmn child. So dev_is_pci() is a constant false for every DRM device
+ * on FreeBSD, PCI or not.
+ *
+ * What separates the two cases is ANCESTRY, not the device's own class:
+ *
+ *	amd64, i915:   pci0 -> vgapci0 -> drmn0
+ *	arm64, vc4:    ofwbus0 -> simplebus1 -> vc40
+ *
+ * so walk up until a PCI bus turns up. A device-tree device never reaches one,
+ * which is what keeps the container_of below out of reach for it.
+ */
+static bool
+drm_dev_has_pci_parent(struct device *dev)
+{
+	device_t bsddev, parent;
+	devclass_t pci_dc;
+
+	if (dev == NULL || (bsddev = dev->bsddev) == NULL)
+		return (false);
+	if ((pci_dc = devclass_find("pci")) == NULL)
+		return (false);
+	for (parent = device_get_parent(bsddev); parent != NULL;
+	    parent = device_get_parent(parent)) {
+		if (device_get_devclass(parent) == pci_dc)
+			return (true);
+	}
+	return (false);
+}
+
 static int
 drm_add_busid_modesetting(struct drm_device *dev, struct sysctl_ctx_list *ctx,
     struct sysctl_oid *top)
 {
 	struct sysctl_oid *oid;
 	int domain, bus, slot, func;
-	struct pci_dev *pdev = to_pci_dev(dev->dev);
+	struct pci_dev *pdev;
 
-	domain = pci_domain_nr(pdev->bus);
-	bus    = pdev->bus->number;
-	slot   = PCI_SLOT(pdev->devfn);
-	func   = PCI_FUNC(pdev->devfn);
+	/*
+	 * to_pci_dev() is a container_of, so calling it on a device that is not
+	 * a PCI function yields a pointer into whatever precedes the caller's
+	 * struct device, and the very next line dereferences it. That is an
+	 * unconditional panic for any non-PCI DRM driver, on the
+	 * drm_dev_register() path -- measured on a Pi 5 bringing up vc4.
+	 *
+	 * A device-tree or platform device has no domain:bus:slot.func to
+	 * report, so name it by its unique string instead, which
+	 * drm_dev_init() has already set from dev_name(parent).
+	 */
+	if (drm_dev_has_pci_parent(dev->dev)) {
+		pdev = to_pci_dev(dev->dev);
 
-	snprintf(dev->busid_str, sizeof(dev->busid_str),
-	    "pci:%04x:%02x:%02x.%d", domain, bus, slot, func);
+		domain = pci_domain_nr(pdev->bus);
+		bus    = pdev->bus->number;
+		slot   = PCI_SLOT(pdev->devfn);
+		func   = PCI_FUNC(pdev->devfn);
+
+		snprintf(dev->busid_str, sizeof(dev->busid_str),
+		    "pci:%04x:%02x:%02x.%d", domain, bus, slot, func);
+	} else {
+		snprintf(dev->busid_str, sizeof(dev->busid_str),
+		    "platform:%s",
+		    dev->unique != NULL ? dev->unique : "unknown");
+	}
 	oid = SYSCTL_ADD_STRING(ctx, SYSCTL_CHILDREN(top), OID_AUTO, "busid",
 	    CTLFLAG_RD, dev->busid_str, 0, NULL);
 	if (oid == NULL)
